# Half-Life's hornet gun, HD rig. Measured by vrcardgen, cut and carded by
# hand.
#
# Part J: "Living weapon: it recharges itself, twitches in the hand, and the
# hornets are visible on it. No hand reload." That is Part K's shock roach
# again - "type `none` with moving parts for show" - so this card takes the
# shock roach's shape, and the first thing it does is NOT DECLARE A TYPE. No
# feed rules, no capacity, no rate, no `fire` line. The recharge is the
# limiter and the recharge is the game's business; an rpm written here would
# be a second timer that refuses to fire when the player has done nothing
# wrong.
#
# WHAT MOVES ON IT. The tool reports ten drivable bones, which is two more
# than VRJ_MAX_JOINTS, so this card has to cut rather than copy the draft.
# Posed through every sequence and measured in the weapon body's own frame
# (`Bip01 Spine1`, which owns 18.8% of the mesh), everything that moves on
# this model is one of four things:
#
#   THREE MANDIBLES, each a two-bone chain hanging off `Bip01 Head`. Two sit
#   below, left and right - `Bone09`->`Bone10` and `Bone05`->`Bone06` - and
#   one sits above on the centreline, `Bone01`->`Bone02`. They carry
#   essentially all of the visible motion: across `Shoot` their tips travel
#   9.16, 9.51 and 5.30 units in the body frame. Across `fidgetSway`, which
#   is the twitch Part J asks for, 11.52, 11.81 and 6.72.
#
#   TWO SLIDING NUBS on the upper flanks, `Dummy07` and `Dummy08`: 18
#   vertices each, 1.51 of pure translation and no rotation at all, in every
#   sequence including `Shoot` (1.13 of it there). They pump; they are the
#   only sliding parts on the weapon.
#
#   TWO SMALL BODIES on the lower flanks, `Bone13` and `Bone15`, 10 vertices
#   each - by size and position, the hornets Part J wants visible on it.
#   Their own channels never move. What moves them is their unskinned parents
#   `Dummy10` and `Dummy09`, which translate 1.35 and 1.28. A joint on either
#   would have no travel of its own to declare, which is why the draft's
#   "EXISTS BUT NEVER ANIMATED" lines are left where the tool put them: in a
#   comment.
#
#   ...and the body, the neck and the head, which are the weapon.
#
# SO: THREE JOINTS, ONE PER MANDIBLE, AT THE ROOT OF EACH CHAIN. The outer
# segment is parented to the root, so driving the root opens the whole limb -
# the same collapse the shock roach's four telescoping segments got, and the
# same parenting the M40A1's bolt body rides on.
#
# Leaving the outer segments to the animation is deliberate, not laziness.
# Nothing in the simulator drives a joint on a weapon with no `type`: recoil
# reaches `action_joint` and this card declares none, so a declared part with
# no rule behind it sits at rest. A card that claimed all six mandible bones
# would make a LIVING weapon stiller than the animation already has it. Three
# roots is the smallest declaration that states the mechanism - three jaws,
# one degree of freedom each, and they open together - while the segments
# riding on them keep twitching until something comes to drive the roots.
#
# THE ROOTS' TRAVELS ARE THE SAME ON THE SD RIG, WHICH MATTERS HERE. SD and
# HD v_hgun share this fingerprint exactly - 21 bones, 6 sequences, the same
# names in the same order - so this one card binds to valve, valve_hd,
# gearbox_hd, bshift and bshift_hd alike. The two rigs disagree only on the
# OUTER segments (`Bone10` 0.933 HD against 0.881 SD, `Bone06` 0.637 against
# 0.593), and those are the two bones this card does not declare. The three
# it does declare measure 0.584, 0.515 and 0.602 on both.
#
# Every rotation here is about the bone's own local z: the other two channels
# move 0.097 at most on the lower pair and not at all on the upper jaw.
#
# NO STRIKE LINE, so it inherits the weak pistol-whip default from
# VRStrike_DefaultCfg, as the shock roach and the displacer do. A hornet gun
# is not something you butt-stroke a grunt with.
#
# NO MUZZLE POINT, because the model has no attachments at all - not unusual
# in this set: 9 of valve_hd's 15 view models ship with none. Nothing in the
# card format wants one; anything that needs a muzzle on this weapon has to
# get it somewhere else.

weapon v_hgun
match  bones 21  seqs 6  hash 0xbd437167

# JOINT 0: the upper jaw, on the centreline above the other two. It turns
# further than either of them (0.602) and it is the one whose opening lives
# at its base rather than its tip - through `Shoot` the root turns 0.366 and
# the segment past it only 0.138, the reverse of the lower pair.
#
# The grab point is the root bone's own measured centroid and the radius is
# half the limb's narrowest measured cross-section in the body frame (3.24
# across). Nothing will ever grab it - there is no hand reload on this weapon
# - but a point on the part beats a point at the origin if anything ever
# draws one.
joint 0  bone "Bone01"  hinge  travel 0.60  rest 0
    grab  point -1.01 0.21 0.29  radius 1.6

# JOINT 1: the lower left jaw. 0.584 of travel, and through `Shoot` the root
# turns 0.201 while `Bone10` past it snaps through 0.671 - this limb opens at
# its knuckle, which is why driving the root carries the flare rather than
# replacing it.
joint 1  bone "Bone09"  hinge  travel 0.58  rest 0
    grab  point 1.69 -2.37 1.12  radius 2.4

# JOINT 2: the lower right jaw, the mirror of joint 1. Measured separately
# and it is NOT a mirror in the numbers - 0.515 against 0.584 at the root,
# and `Bone06` past it 0.637 against `Bone10`'s 0.933. The asymmetry is all
# in `up` and `down`, the draw and the holster; through `Shoot` the two agree
# to a hundredth of a radian (0.201 and 0.209 at the roots, 0.671 and 0.637
# at the tips). Both numbers are the model's, so both go in as measured.
joint 2  bone "Bone05"  hinge  travel 0.51  rest 0
    grab  point -0.62 -2.17 1.13  radius 2.4
